#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaef20bd-2e1c-5084-83b3-6cb7de2c19e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383163} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae8e92c-f3c7-566e-95c7-e2af74b64177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383164} {:answer/id #uuid "1ada8cd6-adb7-5b84-8f4b-f36d29d928e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383165} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8cb3b9-d036-58cb-9784-4aafd495927f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383166} {:answer/id #uuid "777d3f1f-6936-5e4e-994b-118b3c884a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca8cb3b9-d036-58cb-9784-4aafd495927f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383166} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed1c000-f5a1-5681-9f84-e7ad7ca846cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "315ce7db-c9bd-5e7b-89ff-9a198ba7f9b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333801" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71bc4467-5fee-5edc-81ea-6f6e7b365273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383170} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8dc7f9-9328-5356-9480-e8a45926bc09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383171} {:answer/id #uuid "c445bdb2-5211-5e50-af7e-091ca08706bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383172} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e1c109-b971-597a-b97d-4ddd2e2b0720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/a695d58031c47e43d0aed186e271eff7d3da271ea83ae3c053be011afe9d4707.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383173} {:answer/id #uuid "b95654cf-68b4-5ccb-8766-2050ee45a055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/06b9fd74afcc99a3396aa280b248272904c495d6181e6a5c027c0cd6a30acbfb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c445bdb2-5211-5e50-af7e-091ca08706bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383172} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf7e486d-a89d-5302-baff-701223780b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ec66512-f01a-54c5-9b43-60952356c9b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333824" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d84f7f-a204-591f-a881-eb61bea00810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dd3667299cd5da8fe5d9450799fbe865e832449ac39427795a5b87cf2786f587.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383177} {:answer/id #uuid "5376e69d-a7bc-5ac8-ade0-3f9d572db069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/8a7b634d05a938d9b481620da9837a8f28dc28f84ac18a3f9a20678ead0df20b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383178} {:answer/id #uuid "844cd115-ce63-5e18-b11c-f93e3c496aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/59b96e2396db2559e9893c73ef530a312a347442b9c4fde0137583310ceba0a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383179} {:answer/id #uuid "23d3fe90-971e-545d-a2b7-2493f64a7768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f4b88938a280acb1149be29d6832483713437236424bbb640784a55bafea712c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383180} {:answer/id #uuid "684cde0b-3a11-5003-a9c6-60c27e3fd2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "684cde0b-3a11-5003-a9c6-60c27e3fd2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383181} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6ffad53-b39e-533a-8c25-5fc7694782c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1768fec3-139b-5b9c-828f-3c280b299bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-333832" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "805ac354-12f3-5e72-a1c2-8aa423a50826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-334385-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220593} {:answer/id #uuid "a12c5e1a-7842-55ae-99b1-6c36324daed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-334385-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220594} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ceb3f-7e10-55a6-acf1-5259d2ae9141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-334385-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220595} {:answer/id #uuid "de54f244-2097-569e-85db-fbe7ca1389af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-334385-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220596} {:answer/id #uuid "66376028-0b4b-5f63-8a55-99918ce69ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-334385-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66376028-0b4b-5f63-8a55-99918ce69ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6379/Source/Images/q-334385-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220597} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b6762a-1317-5c1a-9d36-ea1d0617e528" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c4bce57-2231-51f6-b0eb-e664a1d75300" :question/math-academy-id "q-334385" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/9988933a3f4393f745b1df6f0dc667a4074f5ee787fab43fb392b117368a8ca9.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/2809f28b49c9782db6fdf3f5a8be7fd99ec6d9a08db8e81d91c4d6bc53efcdda.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7fd67e8a768419dd497bc6345c5999ff921e6de492f60c5f217e93f8dc4d07c1.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bd8fb6-6c17-526f-9aeb-f2d313fbc043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/27610876df35c3b1e8e67e8958a5f28e26dfc26262e55994277629789a4bbd84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383184} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf07c60-9682-566e-ae07-9717c4294078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383185} {:answer/id #uuid "f4494489-6f04-5d26-9f9e-8d84cc3a8dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/4a8d9528c878089370476a45aab127a416a11595ea0b0f972708e5161f208d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383186} {:answer/id #uuid "8740a396-a65b-5613-867d-634185ec2db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383187} {:answer/id #uuid "79a0c238-1caa-503d-84a9-482bd264c9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/15f6170ed7aa922fb1be05914229dd91d98c64879050b15b2302839d419e5177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8740a396-a65b-5613-867d-634185ec2db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383187} :answer-field/id #uuid "5350c3f6-c2cd-514b-a4f3-df186639d4c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50bd4f72-f99e-562e-bd13-7cdd624e464a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334638" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14076166/assets/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)"}]}
